<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tigris</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tigris is one of the two great rivers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, flowing from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> south-east through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. Its waters supported irrigation agriculture and the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. It runs through the history of empires from the Akkadian and Assyrian to the Seleucid, Sasanian and Abbasid, whose capital Baghdad was founded on its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tigris.jpg" alt="Tigris" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Duha masood · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosul_river.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
